# First-boot oneshot: bring the whole stack up with zero manual steps (WI-4.7).
# Runs AFTER docker is up and the network is online.
#
# IT RUNS ON EVERY BOOT, and this comment used to claim otherwise ("becomes a
# no-op on later boots"). `RemainAfterExit=yes` only prevents a second start
# within the SAME boot; there is no ConditionPathExists= here, and the
# /opt/homehub/.provisioned stamp the script writes is read by nothing. That is
# deliberate — the script is idempotent and re-running it is the documented
# repair — but it means any step asking "has this already happened?" must ask it
# of an INPUT, never of its own output. Getting that backwards is how step 3e's
# collision warning came to fire on every reboot (2026-08-03).
#
# WANTS, NOT REQUIRES, ON DOCKER — AND THE DIFFERENCE COST A WHOLE LAB RUN
# (20260809-145118, fixed 2026-08-09). `Requires=` propagates STOPS: stopping
# docker.service stops every unit that Requires it. Step 1b has to stop docker
# to move /var/lib/docker onto its own LV, so this unit was ordering its own
# execution:
#
#   20:21:17  1b: carving /var/lib/docker onto its own LV
#   20:21:17  Logical volume "docker-data" created
#   20:21:18  Main process exited, code=killed, status=15/TERM
#
# It SIGTERMed itself one second in, having created and formatted the LV but
# not mounted it. Nothing after step 1b ran: 0 of 15 image tars loaded, no
# compose stack, no Technitium — so the hub served no DNS, and the panel then
# failed too because it resolves everything by name. Six failing checks, one
# cause.
#
# `After=` is what actually delivers "runs after docker is up"; `Requires=`
# only added "and dies with it". If docker genuinely fails to start, this unit
# now runs anyway and fails loudly at `docker load` — which is the better of
# the two silences.
[Unit]
Description=AWOW always-on core first-boot bring-up (compose + Technitium provisioning)
After=network-online.target docker.service
Wants=network-online.target docker.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/homehub-firstboot.sh
# Generous timeout: first boot pulls images + builds the tracker image.
TimeoutStartSec=1800
# Log to the journal so `journalctl -u homehub-firstboot` shows what happened.
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
